# Cloudflare Pages response headers.
#
# Why this file exists: the update check in app.html has to work from a
# DOWNLOADED copy of the app, opened over file://. Such a page has the origin
# `null`, so its request for version.json is cross-origin, and the browser
# blocks the response unless the server explicitly opts in. Without the header
# below the downloaded copy — the one population that cannot update itself and
# therefore needs the check most — is the one population the check cannot
# reach.
#
# Deliberately scoped to that ONE path. Do not widen this to /* : nothing else
# served here is meant to be readable by another origin, and app.html least of
# all. version.json carries a version string and nothing private, which is the
# whole reason it is safe to open up.
#
# Cache-Control matters as much as the CORS header. A cached version.json
# answers "are you up to date?" with whatever was true the first time it was
# asked, which is exactly the failure the check exists to prevent.
#
# Note for whoever tests this: GitHub Pages ignores _headers entirely. The
# mirror at greenfrog367566.github.io will NOT send these, so a check run
# against it will fail CORS and look broken. Test against menchmark.app.

/version.json
  Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *
  Cache-Control: no-cache, no-store, must-revalidate
